    "lawCode": "Amd §9, Cor L",
    "lawSection": "Correction Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S4061AREVISED 5/14/24\n\nSPONSOR: MANNION\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the correction law, in relation to requiring that\nwebsites providing incarcerated individual information be searchable by\nthe incarcerated individual's name, former name or alias\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to allow the public to be able to search the\nDepartment of Corrections incarcerated individual information database\nby the incarcerated individual's current legal name, their former legal\nname, or any known alias.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSections 1 and 2 of the bill would amend Section 9 of the Correction Law\nto provide that any website that allows the public to search for incar-\ncerated individual information will be programmed in such a way to allow\nfor the public to search by the incarcerated individual's current name,\nany former legal name, or any other known alias of the incarcerated\nindividual.\n\nSection 3 of this bill provides that the effective date shall take\neffect on the ninetieth day after it shall have become a law; provided,\nhowever, that if chapter 631 of the laws of 2023 shall not have taken\neffect on or before such date then section two of this act shall take\neffect on the same date and in the same manner as such chapter of the\nlaws of 2023, takes effect.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nWhen an incarcerated individual legally changes his or her name, the\ngeneral public, and more specifically victims of the incarcerated indi-\nvidual's crime, may not be made aware of this name change. As a result,\nthe public, and more importantly the victim, will no longer be able to\nsearch for the incarcerated individual by the incarcerated individual's\nformer name. This causes significant problems for victims trying to\naccess information on the incarcerated individual.  In order to make\nsearching easier for victims, the website that allows the public to\nsearch for incarcerated individual information should be searchable by\nthe incarcerated individual's current name and any former name. This way\na victim will be able to search for the incarcerated individual by his\nor her former name or alias, if necessary.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2021-22: S.4812-A (Reichlin-Melnick) - Referred to Crime Victims, Crime\nand Correction.\n\n2019-20: S.3536 (Carlucci) - Referred to Crime Victims, Crime and\nCorrection.\n\n2017-18: S.785 (Carlucci) - Passed Senate.\n\n2015-16: S.1745 (Carlucci) - Referred to Crime Victims, Crime and\nCorrection Committee.\n2013-14: S.1209 (Carlucci) - Referred to Crime Victims, Crime and\nCorrection Committee.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
